   230	
   231	    Input structure:
   232	    - A dominant background surrounds several same-colored hollow rectangular rooms with one-cell-thick walls.
   233	    - Some room walls contain continuous missing segments that act as openings.
   234	    - Openings can face each other horizontally or vertically across clear background.
   235	
   236	    Rule:
   237	    - Detect every wall opening as a contiguous missing border segment of a room.
   238	    - Pair two openings only when they face opposite directions, occupy exactly the same row span or column span, and every cell between them is background.
   239	    - For each matched pair, draw a corridor by extending the two wall lines immediately outside the opening span across the gap; keep the corridor interior background.
   240	    - Any opening without a partner is filled back in with the wall color.
   241	
   242	    Key insight:
   243	    - Pairing depends on exact interval alignment plus an unobstructed background gap, not on nearest distance; every unpaired opening must be closed rather than left open.
